\title{MIEB: Massive Image Embedding Benchmark}

\author{Anonymous Author}




\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Image and image-text embeddings are usually evaluated with fragmented, task-specific protocols, which makes it hard to tell whether a model that excels in one setting also generalizes to others. We introduce MIEB, the Massive Image Embedding Benchmark, a unified and reproducible protocol for image and image-text embedding models. MIEB covers 130 tasks in 8 categories and 38 languages, spanning retrieval (including interleaved, multilingual and instruction-aware retrieval), document understanding, linear probing, zero-shot classification, clustering, compositionality, vision-centric question answering and visual semantic textual similarity (STS). Models are evaluated frozen and zero-shot wherever possible; only few-shot linear probes train a light classifier on frozen embeddings. We evaluate 50 models spanning self-supervised, CLIP-style and multimodal-LLM (MLLM) based embedders. No single model dominates: Voyage-multimodal-3 leads the overall mean (62.5), while CLIP-style encoders lead linear probing and zero-shot classification. MLLM-based embeddings are strongest on OCR-heavy and multilingual tasks. We introduce Visual STS, which renders STS sentence pairs as images to probe visual text understanding, and show that Visual STS correlates strongly with MLLM performance on OCR-heavy benchmarks. We further derive MIEB-lite, a 51-task subset that preserves the ranking of the full benchmark (Spearman 0.992) at a fraction of the cost.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Image and image-text embeddings underpin search, recommendation, retrieval and classification. Yet their evaluation remains fragmented: a model is typically judged on zero-shot ImageNet accuracy, on one image-text retrieval set, or on linear probing, and these slices rarely agree. A model that clusters well may fail at multilingual retrieval; a model that retrieves well may read text in images poorly. As a result, there is little consensus on what a universal image or image-text embedding model should be able to do.

Multimodal LLMs (MLLMs) add a further complication. Their quality is often measured by downstream generative benchmarks, which are expensive to run and entangle the embedder with the language model, its instruction tuning and its training data. What is needed is a comprehensive, standardized and efficient benchmark that tests embedding models directly, across vision-only, CLIP-style and MLLM-based embedders.

We present MIEB, which extends the MTEB philosophy~\cite{muennighoff2023mteb} to vision. MIEB unifies 130 tasks in 8 categories and 38 languages under one framework, keeps models frozen, and reports results on a public leaderboard. Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item A unified, large-scale benchmark for image and image-text embeddings (130 tasks, 8 categories, 38 languages), together with a public leaderboard (\autoref{sec:benchmark}).
    \item Visual STS, an image-rendered semantic textual similarity protocol that diagnoses visual text understanding in encoders (\autoref{sec:benchmark}).
    \item MIEB-lite, a 51-task subset derived from inter-task correlations, which retains the ranking of the full benchmark at a large reduction in compute (\autoref{sec:lite}).
    \item An empirical link between encoder scores and MLLM performance, which supports choosing a vision encoder for an MLLM without a full MLLM evaluation (\autoref{sec:discussion}).
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}
\paragraph{Text embedding benchmarks.} MTEB~\cite{muennighoff2023mteb} established a broad, standardized evaluation for text embeddings across task types and languages, and its multilingual extension MMTEB~\cite{enevoldsen2025mmtebmassivemultilingualtext} follows the same design. BEIR~\cite{thakur2021beir} provides heterogeneous zero-shot retrieval evaluation for text. MIEB transfers this design to images and image-text inputs.

\paragraph{Image and image-text embeddings.} CLIP~\cite{radford2021learning} and ALIGN~\cite{jia2021scaling} learn image and text embeddings from language supervision, and SigLIP~\cite{zhai2023sigmoid} replaces the softmax contrastive loss with a sigmoid loss. Scaling studies such as OpenCLIP~\cite{cherti2023reproducible} and DataComp~\cite{gadre2024datacomp}, and EVA-CLIP~\cite{sun2023eva}, improve these encoders. Self-supervised encoders such as MoCo-v3~\cite{chen2021empirical} and DINOv2~\cite{oquab2024dinov2} learn from images alone. Universal multimodal retrievers include UniIR~\cite{wei2023uniir}, VISTA~\cite{zhou2024vista} and GME~\cite{zhang2024gme}; commercial embedders such as Voyage-multimodal-3~\cite{voyagemultimodal2024voyage} are also available.

\paragraph{MLLM-based embeddings.} E5-V~\cite{jiang2024e5} and VLM2Vec~\cite{jiang2024vlm2vec} derive embeddings from multimodal LLMs. Such models are usually evaluated through generative benchmarks, such as those surveyed in Cambrian-1~\cite{tong2024cambrian}, which measure the language model's answers rather than the embedding itself.

\paragraph{Document and text-in-image understanding.} ColPali~\cite{faysse2024colpali} evaluates retrieval over document page images, and multilingual image-text retrieval is commonly measured on XM3600~\cite{thapliyal2022crossmodal}. Compositionality is probed with hard negatives, as in Winoground~\cite{Thrush_2022_CVPR}. Visual STS builds on the sentence similarity literature, including SemEval STS~\cite{agirre-etal-2013-sem,cer-etal-2017-semeval} and sentence embeddings~\cite{reimers2019sentence}.

\section{The MIEB Benchmark}
\label{sec:benchmark}
MIEB covers 130 tasks in 8 categories and 38 languages, summarized in \autoref{tab:MIEB big tasks}, and organized as in \autoref{fig:mieb_tasks}. Retrieval (45 tasks) covers all modality pairings from image-to-image through interleaved image-text, including multilingual and instruction-aware settings. Document understanding (10 tasks, 2 languages) uses the retrieval protocol on OCR-heavy document data. Linear probing (22 tasks), zero-shot classification (23 tasks), and clustering (5 tasks) probe classification-style abilities. Compositionality (7 tasks) is a pair classification task, and vision-centric QA (6 tasks) is evaluated as retrieval. Visual STS (9 tasks, 12 languages in the English and multilingual subsets) is described below. The per-category counts in \autoref{tab:MIEB big tasks} sum to 127 rather than 130; the source material does not assign the remaining three tasks to a category, and we report the counts as given.

