\paragraph{Data and annotation.} Tasks are curated from established datasets, including multilingual retrieval (xFlickr\&CO, XM3600, WIT), document understanding (VIDORE-style tasks), compositionality (ARO, Winoground), classification (ImageNet-1k and fine-grained sets), and STS. MIEB collects no new human annotations: it reuses the labels of the source datasets. Standard formats, dataset and model versioning, and a single metadata file per dataset make the protocol reproducible and easy to extend.

\paragraph{Evaluation protocols.} Retrieval ranks documents by similarity to a query and reports nDCG@10, or nDCG@5 for document understanding; recall@1 or MAP@5 is used where appropriate. Zero-shot classification maps labels to prompts such as ``a photo of \{label\}'' and reports top-1 accuracy. Linear probing trains a logistic regression (at most 100 iterations) on frozen embeddings using 16 shots per class by default and reports accuracy. Clustering runs k-means with $k$ equal to the number of ground-truth classes and reports normalized mutual information (NMI). Compositionality reports accuracy on positives against hard negatives built by perturbing attributes, relations or word order. Visual STS correlates embedding similarity of rendered sentence pairs with human scores and reports Spearman $\rho$ at the dataset level.

\paragraph{Interleaved inputs and multilinguality.} MLLM-based models embed interleaved image-text inputs natively. For other encoders, an interleaved representation is formed by summing the image and text embeddings. Multilingual results are reported as both the mean and the variance across languages.

\paragraph{Visual STS.} STS12--17 and STS-b sentence pairs are rendered as images, so a vision encoder must read the text before comparing meanings. The human similarity labels are inherited from STS and no new annotation is collected. Visual STS therefore isolates a capability, reading text in images and mapping it to meaning, that is absent from text-only STS. Rendering choices and font layout vary, and we discuss this in the limitations.

