\section{Models}
We evaluate 50 models in three families: vision-only self-supervised models (MoCo-v3, DINOv2); CLIP-style language-supervised models (CLIP, OpenCLIP, SigLIP including the multilingual variant, ALIGN, DataComp-CLIP, EVA-CLIP, VISTA); and MLLM-based embedders (E5-V, VLM2Vec in full and LoRA variants, and the commercial Voyage-multimodal-3). The per-category results of the top 20 models by mean are given in \autoref{tab: overall results top 20.}.

\section{Implementation Details}
MIEB is implemented on the MTEB codebase, and every model and dataset is versioned in the repository and on the leaderboard. Adding a model requires a wrapper that produces image and text embeddings; adding a dataset requires a metadata entry. Runtime figures in this paper are measured in H100 GPU hours. Linear probes use the frozen embeddings with the logistic regression described above, and all other tasks are zero-shot.

\section{Experimental Results}
\paragraph{Overall results.} Voyage-multimodal-3 has the highest overall mean on MIEB (62.5), followed by E5-V (58.2) and SigLIP-so400m-patch14-384 (57.1). Category leaders differ: CLIP-family models lead linear probing (86.0, EVA02-CLIP-bigE-14-plus) and zero-shot classification (70.8, SigLIP-so400m-patch14-384 and EVA02-CLIP-bigE-14-plus), and clustering (NMI 92.4, EVA02-CLIP-bigE-14-plus). Retrieval is led by CLIP-ViT-bigG-14-laion2B (41.5 average over 45 tasks), with SigLIP-so400m close behind (40.8). \autoref{tab: overall results top 20.} gives the per-category breakdown.

\paragraph{Document understanding and visual text.} On document understanding, Voyage-multimodal-3 reaches nDCG@5 71.1 and E5-V the best open-source score, 62.7. On Visual STS (English average over 7 sets), Voyage scores 81.8 and E5-V 79.3. CLIP-family encoders also achieve high Visual STS scores.

\paragraph{Compositionality and vision-centric QA.} Compositionality is hard for all models. The best score is 46.3, achieved by E5-V, and WinoGround is particularly difficult; the overview attributes this to image and text confounders, but gives no direct evidence for that cause. On vision-centric QA, VLM2Vec-Full (62.1) and VLM2Vec-LoRA (62.0) lead.

\paragraph{Multilingual retrieval.} \autoref{tab: multilingual retrieval} reports multilingual retrieval averaged over 38 languages, with variance across languages. E5-V averages 74.3 with a variance of 1.4, the highest average, and SigLIP averages 66.8 with variance 2.6. OpenCLIP and EVA02-CLIP average about 31 with high variance (7.3 and 7.2), so their multilingual performance depends strongly on the language.

