\section{Discussions}
\label{sec:discussion}
\paragraph{CLIP-style versus MLLM-based embedders.} No single model dominates. CLIP-style encoders, especially at scale with high-quality data, excel at linear probing, zero-shot classification, clustering and many retrieval tasks. They lag on interleaved retrieval, OCR-heavy visual text understanding and multilingual tasks unless specialized, as SigLIP-multilingual is. MLLM-based embedders perform well on document understanding, Visual STS, instruction-aware and multilingual retrieval, which require robust visual-text fusion, but they are weaker on fine-grained classification and clustering, which suggests less precise low-level visual granularity.

\paragraph{Visual STS as a proxy for text-in-image ability.} E5-V's Visual STS score tracks its text-only STS score (79.3 against 86.0 on average), as \autoref{tab:E5-V STS analysis} shows, which indicates near-OCR-level reading with residual gaps. The per-dataset gap is largest on STS13 (89.9 against 78.2) and smallest on STS17 (87.9 against 85.8). \autoref{fig:correlation} reports correlations between Visual STS sets and MLLM benchmark scores. For OCR-heavy benchmarks (OCRBench, TextVQA) the coefficients are 0.97--0.99, which suggests Visual STS could help choose a vision encoder for such MLLM tasks; the figure does not establish this for other benchmarks.

