\section{Limitations}
MIEB focuses on images and image-text inputs; video and audio are out of scope. Visual STS depends on rendering choices and font layout, and harder settings, such as noisy scans or handwriting, are future work. Document understanding is evaluated only through retrieval-style tasks, so generative or structured extraction tasks could capture complementary skills. Interleaved inputs for encoder-only models use summed embeddings, which may understate what those models can do; more principled fusion is future work. Some domains, such as 3D and spatial reasoning, are lightly represented, and vision-centric QA and compositionality could be expanded. The category counts in \autoref{tab:MIEB big tasks} do not sum to the total number of tasks (see \autoref{sec:benchmark}), and this should be resolved in a future release.

\section{Conclusion}
MIEB provides a unified, reproducible benchmark for image and image-text embeddings, with 130 tasks in 8 categories and 38 languages, plus the 51-task MIEB-lite for efficient evaluation. The results show complementary strengths across model families: CLIP-style encoders excel at fine-grained vision tasks, and MLLM-based embedders lead on multilingual, interleaved and OCR-heavy tasks. Visual STS offers a cheap diagnostic of visual text understanding that correlates with MLLM OCR-heavy performance, which makes MIEB a practical tool for selecting and improving universal multimodal embedders.

